      // sc-20799 gap 1: the Candle/CUDA Bernini STILL-image memory ladder, previously undeclared.
      // `provider` is `bernini`, not `bernini_image`: the MODEL_TABLE row maps sceneworks id
      // `bernini_image` onto `engine_id: "bernini"`, and `evaluate_shared_image` looks the
      // declaration up by ENGINE id. That is `crate::bernini::MODEL_ID` in candle-gen-bernini, i.e.
      // the provider behind FULL_MEMORY_REGISTRATION (the sibling `bernini_renderer` /
      // `crate::pipeline::MODEL_ID` provider is the renderer-only route and is not this entry).
      //
      // Engine truth at pinned inference ebcdc7da79612d19dfe1dbc748c55a9ffe8336e6,
      // crates/media/candle-gen/candle-gen-bernini/src/memory_strategy.rs:
      //  - `strategies()` marks Resident and BoundedDecode Implemented and leaves StagedResidency,
      //    BoundedAttention and BoundedTransformerResidency MISSING. The file header states why, and
      //    it is a deliberate non-inheritance from the MLX claims: the CUDA provider has a real tiled
      //    Wan z16 VAE decode seam, but the older unconditional phase staging is NOT a
      //    request-selected StagedResidency lever, and Bernini has no Candle deferred transformer
      //    loader or attention-chunking seam. Missing is not StructurallyNotApplicable, so those
      //    three rungs are simply absent below — no structural-exemption block is warranted.
      //  - BoundedDecode parameters come straight from the crate: `DECODE_TILE_EDGES`
      //    [512, 448, 384, 320, 256, 192] and `DECODE_OVERLAP` 64, kept in lockstep with the merged
      //    Wan z16 implementation. `validate_decode` rejects any edge outside that list or any
      //    overlap but 64.
      //  - Still route (`route_ok`, mode key "text_to_image" | "edit"): reference_count must be
      //    exactly 0 for text_to_image and exactly 1 for edit, batch 1, frames 1, no PiD, no phases,
      //    and the geometry must be one of `ADVERTISED_STILL_GEOMETRIES`
      //    [(512,512), (768,768), (1024,1024), (1280,720), (720,1280)] — which is byte-for-byte this
      //    entry's `limits.resolutions` above, so the catalog cannot present an inadmissible frame.
      //    The overlay must be absent or exactly "lora".
      //  - `expected_packing` accepts None/Q4/Q8, giving the bf16/q8/q4 published tier subdirs the
      //    worker's `resolve_candle_bernini_tier_dir_and_quant` selects.
      //  - The "lora" overlay is declared because the off-Mac Candle descriptor really does serve
      //    LoRA/LoKr (see this entry's `loraCompatibility` note: the empty `types` list keeps the
      //    global MLX picker from exposing a no-op, while the capability matrix preserves the exact
      //    Candle-only LoRA/LoKr cells). `resolve_adapters` on the bernini still lane installs that
      //    stack through the generator load spec.
      //  - ⚠️ FINGERPRINT SCOPE — read before treating these rows as measurement. The PRODUCTION
      //    registration (`FULL_MEMORY_REGISTRATION` -> `memory_strategy_contract`) builds a contract
      //    with `calibration: None`; the crate's own test asserts `contract.calibration == None`,
      //    because production calibration is deliberately absent until the Windows/CUDA real-weight
      //    campaign exists. There is therefore NO captured still-lane calibration identity to quote.
      //    The authoring schema nevertheless REQUIRES `fingerprint` on every hand-authored
      //    (`source: "inference:…"`) row — only generator-written
      //    `config/engine-capabilities/…` rows may omit it — so these rows carry the crate's single
      //    calibration constant `STATIC_CALIBRATION`, verbatim and unmodified. Two caveats it must
      //    not be read past: that constant is attached to the WEIGHTS-FREE contract
      //    (`weights_free_memory_strategy_contract`), not to production loads; and its "rv2v" name is
      //    historical — the constant is applied route-independently for both Bernini providers, not
      //    only to the reference-video-to-video route. It stands here as the declaration's identity
      //    for the pinned provider code, NOT as evidence that this still route has been calibrated.
      //    Replace it with a real still-lane identity when that campaign mints one.
      "candle": {

      },
      "ui": {
        "label": "Bernini (Image)",
        "description": "ByteDance Bernini full pipeline as a still-image generator: a Qwen2.5-VL semantic planner driving a Wan2.2 A14B renderer to produce a single image (text-to-image) or edit a source image (image-to-image). Highest-quality, planner-guided — but very large (≈179 GB) and slow (the 7B planner runs many passes per image). Native MLX on macOS and Candle on Windows/Linux; needs ample accelerator memory. For everyday stills the fast native image models are recommended.",
        "recommendedFor": ["text_to_image", "edit_image"],
        "promptGuide": {
          "title": "Bernini Image Prompt Guide",
          "path": "/prompt-guides/bernini-image.md",
          "sources": [
            { "label": "Bernini full model card", "url": "https://huggingface.co/ByteDance/Bernini-Diffusers" },
            { "label": "Bernini GitHub repo", "url": "https://github.com/bytedance/Bernini" }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    // SCAIL-2 (epic 5439): zai-org's end-to-end controlled character-animation /
    // motion-transfer model. Give a reference character image + a driving video and
    // SCAIL-2 animates the character with the driving motion. Backbone is Wan2.1-14B
    // I2V (dense) + the stock Wan2.1 z16 VAE / umt5-xxl + an open-CLIP XLM-RoBERTa
    // ViT-H/14 image tower; packed-token conditioning with auto-generated color-coded
    // segmentation masks (the worker paints them from native SAM3 — no user masks).
    // Native MLX on macOS and native Candle on NVIDIA Windows/Linux (engine id "scail2_14b").
    // The verified q4/q8/bf16 package tiers are shared by both native backends. Distributed the
    // SceneWorks/*-mlx turnkey way: a pre-quantized Q4 snapshot is
    // published as `SceneWorks/scail2-mlx` (sc-5445), downloaded on first use. This entry
    // covers the standalone `animate_character` mode (sc-5447 /
    // sc-5448) AND cross-identity `replace_person` (sc-5452 — the SAME engine with
    // replace_flag=true, offered as the higher-quality backend behind the existing
    // person-track replacement pipeline). LoRA refinement under sc-5451 — not declared
    // until wired.
    {
      "id": "scail2_14b",
      "name": "SCAIL-2",
      "family": "scail2",
      "type": "video",
      "adapter": "scail2",
      "capabilities": [
        "animate_character",
        "replace_person"
      ],
      "downloads": [
        // Quant matrix (sc-9944, epic 8506): each tier is a SEPARATE user-selectable artifact
        // tagged with a `variant`; `q4` (default) installs when no tier is chosen. Each tier subdir is
        // fully self-contained (the DiT `dit.safetensors` at the tier's precision + the shared dense
        // Wan2.1 z16 VAE + UMT5 T5 encoder + open-CLIP ViT-H/14 visual tower + tokenizer + config.json
        // with the quant baked in), so the dense VAE/T5/CLIP repeat per tier (epic decision #3: storage
        // is a non-issue; keeps the load path a single self-contained dir with no engine change). On
        // macOS the worker fetches a toggled-but-not-downloaded tier on demand
        // (ensure_scail2_tier_present, video_jobs.rs); off-Mac Model Manager provisions the selected
        // exact variant. The legacy flat root files
        // (the shipped Q4 snapshot + the DPO LoRA pickle) stay hosted for already-shipped workers that
        // resolve the repo root; a new worker only ever resolves the tier subdirs.
        // estimatedSizeBytes/footprint are the exact hosted subdir sizes measured post-build.
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/scail2-mlx",
          "revision": "ce88cfdb1008f395e9c820e525e6db7b6695f7b3",
          "variant": "q4",
          "default": true,
          "files": ["q4/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          // The dense T5 (~11.4 GB) + open-CLIP tower (~2.5 GB) are shared bf16 in every tier, so the
          // Q4 DiT (~9.6 GB) makes this only modestly smaller than q8/bf16 (same shape as SD3.5).
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 23993093306,
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 23993093306 }
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/scail2-mlx",
          "revision": "ce88cfdb1008f395e9c820e525e6db7b6695f7b3",
          "variant": "q8",
          "files": ["q8/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 32067131269,
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 32067131269 }
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/scail2-mlx",
          "revision": "ce88cfdb1008f395e9c820e525e6db7b6695f7b3",
          "variant": "bf16",
          "files": ["bf16/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          // Dense bf16 tier (sc-9944): the DiT as a true dense mirror of the zai-org fp32 checkpoint
          // (reconstructed for this story; the pre-sc-5445 dense snapshot had been pruned) + the shared
          // dense VAE/T5/CLIP/tokenizer. Exact on-disk size of the built bf16/ subdir.
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 47205846839,
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 47205846839 }
        }
      ],
      "paths": {
        "model": "${HF_CACHE}/SceneWorks/scail2-mlx"
      },
      "defaults": {
        "duration": 5,
        "fps": 16,
        "resolution": "832x480",
        "quality": "balanced",
        "sampler": "default",
        "scheduler": "default"
      },
      "limits": {
        // One 81-frame driving segment ≈ 5s at 16fps (longer clips stitch segments —
        // each is a full 14B denoise, so keep clips short). Core aligns W×H to a
        // multiple of 32 — mlx's hardcoded `DIM_ALIGN = 32` (mlx-gen-scail2/src/generate.rs:73),
        // which core's default floor already matches, so no stride is declared. Engine
        // max_size 1280. The output bucket should match the driving clip's aspect (the
        // clip sets the motion + framing).
        //
        // Area: candle shares the 14B envelope — `reject_over_area` rejects above MAX_AREA_14B
        // (candle-gen-scail2/src/pipeline.rs:294), added because `max_size` alone only bounds each
        // EDGE, so 1280×1280 slipped through. That cap is now the 14B family's real 921,600, up
        // from the TI2V-5B's 901,120 it had borrowed (sc-12308).
        //
        // Raising it does NOT give SCAIL-2 720p, and that is expected: its buckets stay 704-tall
        // because of its own `DIM_ALIGN = 32` tiling grid above — 720 is off THAT lattice, an
        // unrelated constraint from the area. 1280x704 (901,120) remains its largest legal bucket
        // (the next 32-aligned step, 1280x736, is 942,080 — over the cap).
        "durations": [3, 4, 5],
        "recommendedMaxDuration": 5,
        "hardMaxDuration": 5,
        "fps": [16],
        "maxPixels": 921600,
        "resolutions": ["832x480", "480x832", "1280x704", "704x1280"],
        "samplers": ["default"],
        "schedulers": ["default"]
      },
      "loraCompatibility": { "sizeClass": "14b", "imageConditionedBackbone": true,
        // SCAIL-2 LoRA is wired (sc-5451, mlx-gen #462): the engine descriptor reports
        // supports_lora/supports_lokr and installs a standard lora_down/up adapter as a
        // forward-time residual over the Q4 base; the worker routes request.loras via
        // resolve_scail2_adapters (sc-5686). Covers the bundled Bias-Aware DPO quality
        // (enhance) LoRA and user scail2-family LoRAs. The lightx2v diff-patch "lightning"
        // LoRA (sc-5684, mlx-gen #465) installs via the engine's in-place diff-patch merge
        // and is bundled as the `scail2_lightning` speed toggle (sc-5700, builtin.loras.jsonc)
        // — selecting it auto-applies the 8-step / CFG-off recipe.
        "families": ["scail2"],
        "types": ["style", "motion", "character", "enhance"]
      },
      "mlx": {
        // Turnkey pre-converted MLX repo (no requiresConversion). The worker resolves
        // env override (SCENEWORKS_MLX_SCAIL2_DIR) → local <data>/models/mlx/scail2 →
        // this download-on-first-use snapshot, mirroring bernini.
        // minMemoryGb MEASURED under sc-5681 (mlx-gen #463). The real production bucket is
        // 832x480/5s (81-frame segment): the full pipeline runs f32 DiT compute (bf16
        // overflowed to NaN at that sequence length) and a temporal-tiled VAE decode, with a
        // measured process footprint of ~76 GB (MLX-active peaks: preproc 13.5 / denoise 24.8 /
        // decode 33.2 GB; the Metal VAE-decode conv scratch is ~2x the active). 76 GB exceeds
        // 64, so 96 is the floor that fits with headroom (the earlier provisional 48 only ever
        // ran the 256² smoke, which never exercised a real bucket).

        "repo": "SceneWorks/scail2-mlx",
        "quantize": 4,
        "limits": {
          "samplers": ["default"],
          "schedulers": ["default"]
        }
      },
      // Exact shared-package Candle/CUDA evidence. The bf16 row is the sc-18473 production capture;
      // epic 20738 terminal artifact 9492288293 adds q4/q8 at the identical 832x480 output geometry.
      // q4 uses the larger of its ordinary and six-reference cells (62,730 MiB); q8 peaked at
      // 66,486 MiB. The default q4 blanket is ceil(61.260 + the shared 2 GiB reserve) = 64.
      // The earlier bf16 evidence used inference
      // 3174984b20334bb029170e367be234de0b3f8753 loaded the Model Manager bf16 directory from
      // SceneWorks/scail2-mlx@ce88cfdb1008f395e9c820e525e6db7b6695f7b3 through the production
      // candle_gen_scail2 provider and rendered 832x480 / 81 frames / 1 step. GitHub Actions run
      // 31455292141, job 93667700921, artifact 9089420126 (uploaded zip SHA-256
      // de62f67b175ca91519602d5e024baf5342907b9fbe8d1297ad5abb561748bac9) emitted the validated
      // overallPeakGb=102.115 marker after proving a stable 0.020 GB idle baseline.
      "candle": {

      },
      "ui": {
        "label": "SCAIL-2",
        "description": "zai-org SCAIL-2 character animation: give ordered reference character images and a driving video, and SCAIL-2 animates the supplied characters with the driving motion (the driving clip sets the motion and timing). Built on Wan2.1-14B; native MLX on macOS and native Candle on NVIDIA Windows/Linux admit the same installable q4, q8, and bf16 package variants when installed. Measured Candle floors are 64 GB for q4, 67 GB for q8, and 105 GB for bf16. Runs minutes-per-clip (segmented 14B denoise).",
        "recommendedFor": ["animate_character"],
        "durationHint": "Segmented 14B denoise — keep clips at 5s or less. Generates at 16fps; the driving clip sets the motion and timing.",
        "promptGuide": {
          "title": "SCAIL-2 Prompt Guide",
          "path": "/prompt-guides/scail2.md",
          "sources": [
            { "label": "SCAIL-2 model card", "url": "https://huggingface.co/zai-org/SCAIL-2" },
            { "label": "SCAIL-2 GitHub repo", "url": "https://github.com/zai-org/SCAIL-2" }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    // Krea Realtime 14B (epic 8431, sc-8444 S11): krea/krea-realtime-video, Apache-2.0 — the
    // Wan 2.1 T2V 14B backbone distilled via Self-Forcing into an AUTOREGRESSIVE generator. It
    // renders frame-blocks left-to-right against a rolling causal KV cache at ~6 few-step
    // denoises per block instead of one whole-clip diffusion, which is why it is its own engine
    // (mlx-gen-krea-realtime) rather than a Wan variant. macOS-only native MLX; there is no
    // candle path (the worker fails a non-mac job loudly, `video_jobs/mod.rs`).
    //
    // Distinct from the existing `krea_*` IMAGE lane (Krea 2 Turbo / Raw, engine `mlx-gen-krea`).
    // Same vendor, unrelated architecture — do not merge their ids, families, or LoRA families.
    {
      "id": "krea_realtime_14b",
      "name": "Krea Realtime 14B",
      "family": "krea-realtime",
      "type": "video",
      "adapter": "krea_realtime",
      // The engine routes purely on the conditioning the worker hands it (`pipeline::run`):
      // nothing → t2v, a Reference still → i2v (VAE-encoded to warm the AR KV cache), a
      // VideoClip source → v2v (a strength-controlled AR init). Those are exactly the three
      // capabilities declared here; the descriptor advertises no first/last-frame, clip-extend,
      // bridge, or person-replace surface, so none is claimed.
      "capabilities": [
        "text_to_video",
        "image_to_video",
        "video_to_video"
      ],
      // Which generation AXES this engine actually has (sc-8445), the video-lane sibling of the
      // `audio` sub-block. Krea Realtime is CFG-FREE: the Self-Forcing distillation baked guidance
      // out, so `generate_krea_realtime` runs a single batch-1 forward, sets `negative_prompt: None`
      // and never forwards a guidance scale (video_jobs/krea_realtime.rs). Declaring that here lets
      // Video Studio HIDE both controls instead of offering knobs the worker discards — and makes
      // this entry's own `ui.description` ("CFG-off, so there is no negative prompt or guidance
      // control") a true statement about the shipped UI rather than an aspiration.
      //
      // ⚠️ Polarity is the OPPOSITE of the `audio` block's: an ABSENT key here means TRUE. Of the
      // ten video entries, EIGHT genuinely take both (the Wan family, both LTX entries, SCAIL-2 and
      // Bernini) and declare nothing; the two that do not — this entry and `svd`, whose engine
      // drives its own frame-wise CFG ramp — declare it false right here. So absent-means-supported
      // is the correct default for the majority AND leaves those eight behaving exactly as they did
      // before this block existed. The schema says the same thing.
      "video": {
        "supportsSchedulerShift": false,
        "supportsGuidance": false,
        "supportsNegativePrompt": false
      },
      "downloads": [
        // macOS quant matrix (epic 8506 shape, mirroring scail2_14b): each tier is a SEPARATE
        // user-selectable artifact tagged with a `variant`; `q4` (default) installs when no tier
        // is chosen. Every tier subdir is fully self-contained — the Krea DiT at that tier's
        // precision plus the STOCK Wan 2.1 companions the engine reuses verbatim (z16 VAE, UMT5
        // text encoder, tokenizer) and a `config.json` carrying the AR fields — so the dense
        // companions repeat per tier and a tier dir is a complete `from_snapshot` tree.
        // Companions are byte-identical across all three tiers: t5_encoder 11,361,845,504 ·
        // vae 507,591,212 · tokenizer 16,837,417. Sizes below are the exact hosted byte counts
        // read back from the published repo tree, so `estimatedSizeBytes` is not an estimate.
        //
        // ⚠️ `files` are EXPLICIT PATHS, deliberately not a `<tier>/*` glob. The worker's glob
        // (`imports.rs::pattern_matches`, Rust `glob` with default MatchOptions) lets `*` cross
        // `/`, so `bf16/*` WOULD reach the nested `bf16/transformer/` shards — the download
        // itself is not the exposure. The exposure is CACHE HEALTH: `snapshot_contains_pattern`
        // (apps/rust-api/src/models.rs) treats a glob as satisfied by ANY one matching file, and
        // this repo ships no `model_index.json`, so the diffusers per-component augmentation is
        // a no-op for it — a tier that lost 6 of its 7 DiT shards would still read `installed`
        // and then die at load. Explicit paths make every file its own presence check (exact
        // `path_is_readable_file`) and its own download-time claim (sc-12283's per-pattern
        // hard-fail), so a torn tier reports `incomplete` and names the missing shard.
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/krea-realtime-14b-mlx",
          "revision": "e68e9a3d98187fdf6936838ffcf6df5aa48d6626",
          "variant": "q4",
          "default": true,
          "files": [
            "q4/config.json",
            "q4/dit.safetensors",
            "q4/t5_encoder.safetensors",
            "q4/tokenizer.json",
            "q4/vae.safetensors"
          ],
          "platforms": ["macos"],
          // Q4 DiT 8,378,982,809 + the shared dense companions. The default tier for this dense
          // 14B video engine (sc-10750's dense-video convention) — a bf16 DiT is ~28 GB resident
          // before the AR KV cache, so the quantized tiers are the practically usable ones.
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 20265258499,
          // MEASURED on-device by sc-8446 (S13), Apple M5 Max, 832x480 / 81 frames / 24 fps, the
          // shipped Self-Forcing schedule. `resident` is the steady-state AR-loop MLX-active
          // plateau (Q4 DiT ~7.8 GiB + the 7.14 GiB KV cache + the z16 VAE + per-step
          // activations); `peak` is the whole-run MLX-active high-water mark, which the terminal
          // VAE decode sets (+10.5 GiB over the AR loop). Both are MLX **active** — the
          // allocator's reclaimable buffer cache took the same run to ~90 GiB and is deliberately
          // not counted (see mlx.minMemoryGb).
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 20265258499 }
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/krea-realtime-14b-mlx",
          "revision": "e68e9a3d98187fdf6936838ffcf6df5aa48d6626",
          "variant": "q8",
          "files": [
            "q8/config.json",
            "q8/dit.safetensors",
            "q8/t5_encoder.safetensors",
            "q8/tokenizer.json",
            "q8/vae.safetensors"
          ],
          "platforms": ["macos"],
          // Q8 DiT 15,404,443,762 + the shared dense companions.
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 27290719452,
          // DERIVED from the measured Q4 figures by weight-size substitution (the only term that
          // changes between tiers): the Q8 DiT is 15,404,443,762 B vs Q4's 8,378,982,809, i.e.
          // +6.54 GiB resident. The KV cache does NOT change — it holds bf16 activations, so it is
          // 7.14 GiB on every tier. Marked derived, not measured: only Q4 was run on-device.
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 27290719452 }
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/krea-realtime-14b-mlx",
          "revision": "e68e9a3d98187fdf6936838ffcf6df5aa48d6626",
          "variant": "bf16",
          // The bf16 DiT is SHARDED one level deeper (`bf16/transformer/dit-0000N-of-00007`),
          // because a single 28.58 GB bf16 file is above the practical single-blob limit the
          // rehost emits; the q4/q8 DiTs are packed small enough to stay flat as `dit.safetensors`.
          // The engine handles either layout (`t2v::open_transformer_weights` takes a flat
          // `dit.safetensors` OR a `transformer/` dir), so this is a hosting shape, not a second
          // load path. All 7 shards are listed individually — see the glob note above.
          "files": [
            "bf16/config.json",
            "bf16/t5_encoder.safetensors",
            "bf16/tokenizer.json",
            "bf16/vae.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00001-of-00007.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00002-of-00007.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00003-of-00007.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00004-of-00007.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00005-of-00007.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00006-of-00007.safetensors",
            "bf16/transformer/dit-00007-of-00007.safetensors"
          ],
          "platforms": ["macos"],
          // 7 DiT shards totalling 28,577,095,227 + the shared dense companions.
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 40463370856,
          // DERIVED the same way: the bf16 DiT is 28,577,095,227 B, i.e. +18.81 GiB over Q4.
          // This tier is what sets `mlx.minMemoryGb` — its ~47 GiB active peak is the largest any
          // installable tier reaches, and a single per-model floor has to admit it.
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 40463370856 }
        }
      ],
      "paths": {
        "model": "${HF_CACHE}/SceneWorks/krea-realtime-14b-mlx"
      },
      "defaults": {
        // 24 fps: the model's own cadence (the card's reference driver exports at
        // `fps=24`), NOT the 16 the rehost's emitted `config.json` inherits from Wan.
        // See the limits note for why 24 is also the only cadence that keeps every
        // advertised duration on the AR block lattice.
        "duration": 4,
        "fps": 24,
        "resolution": "832x480",
        "quality": "balanced",
        // 6 few-step denoises per frame-block — the reference driver's own
        // `num_inference_steps=6`, exposed through the Advanced steps control.
        "steps": 6,
        "sampler": "default",
        "scheduler": "default"
      },
      "limits": {
        // ---- fps + duration lattice (DERIVED, not inherited) --------------------------------
        // Three facts compose into the lattice:
        //   1. The z16 Wan VAE is 4x temporal, so an output frame count must satisfy
        //      `frames = 4k + 1` (core's `wan_frame_count` FLOORS to the largest such value).
        //   2. The AR loop consumes `num_frames_per_block = 3` LATENT frames per block, and a
        //      latent count is `(frames - 1) / 4 + 1`. A whole number of blocks therefore wants
        //      that latent count to be a multiple of 3 — i.e. `frames = 12k - 3`. A partial
        //      trailing block is handled (`generate.rs::chunk_frame_counts`), it is just wasted
        //      work rendering frames the clip then trims.
        //   3. Krea Realtime is a 24 fps model.
        // At 24 fps, `wan_frame_count(24 * d) = 24d - 3 = 12(2d) - 3` for every integer d — so
        // EVERY advertised duration lands on an exact 2d-block boundary: 2s -> 45 frames / 4
        // blocks, 3s -> 69 / 6, 4s -> 93 / 8, 5s -> 117 / 10. At the config's inherited 16 fps
        // it does not: 4s -> 61 frames -> 16 latent frames, which is 5 blocks plus a 1-frame
        // remainder. That is the derivation, and `krea_realtime_durations_are_on_the_ar_block_
        // lattice` (video_request.rs) pins it.
        //
        // `hardMaxDuration` 5 is a stability ceiling, not a memory one: the shipped checkpoint
        // runs `sink_size = 0`, so a bounded-window AR clip drifts as it lengthens (sc-15127
        // tracks the non-zero-sink fix). 5s / 10 blocks is just past the reference driver's own
        // 9-block example.
        "durations": [2, 3, 4, 5],
        "recommendedMaxDuration": 5,
        "hardMaxDuration": 5,
        "fps": [24],
        // ---- geometry -----------------------------------------------------------------------
        // Stride 16 = the z16 Wan 2.1 VAE's 8px spatial stride x the DiT's patch 2, the same
        // lattice the rest of the Wan-14B family renders on. The engine derives its per-frame
        // token count from the REQUESTED size (`t2v::resolve_request_config` recomputes
        // `frame_seq_length` from the latent dims), so the reference's baked 1560 (= 52x30 for
        // 832x480) is a default, not a constraint — every bucket below is genuinely renderable.
        //
        // NO `maxPixels`: unlike the Wan/bernini/scail2 14B engines, mlx-gen-krea-realtime
        // declares no area check — its descriptor bounds each EDGE (`max_size: 1280`) and
        // gen-core's `validate_request` has no area term. A model with no engine cap must not
        // invent one (the `ENGINE_GEOMETRY` contract), so this stays absent rather than
        // borrowing the 14B family's 921,600.
        //
        // 832x480 is the reference bucket and the default. The 720p pair is legal geometry and
        // renders, but it is ~2.3x the tokens per frame and therefore ~2.3x the AR KV cache
        // (~17.7 GB vs ~7.7 GB bf16) — see mlx.minMemoryGb.
        "requiresDimensionsMultipleOf": 16,
        "resolutions": ["832x480", "480x832", "1280x720", "720x1280"],
        // Sealed sampler surface: the engine advertises exactly one sampler, its fixed
        // Self-Forcing few-step renoise schedule (`SELF_FORCING_SAMPLER`), and no scheduler
        // axis at all. There is nothing to choose, so this declares the sealed-adapter
        // `["default"]` (the schema's own convention) rather than inventing a `self_forcing`
        // menu entry the worker would never send — it passes `sampler: None` / `scheduler: None`.
        "samplers": ["default"],
        "schedulers": ["default"]
      },
      "loraCompatibility": { "sizeClass": "14b", "imageConditionedBackbone": false,
        // Krea Realtime gets its OWN family token. The DiT is Wan 2.1 T2V 14B weight-for-weight,
        // so Wan-family style/motion LoRAs do install on it (sc-15015 wired the dense
        // forward-time residual path, tier-agnostic over the packed Q4/Q8 bases) — but declaring
        // `wan-video` HERE would make this entry a member of the Wan family for every other
        // family-keyed gate too (training bases, tier layout, repo resolution), which it is not.
        // The one-directional "also accepts wan-video LoRAs" relation belongs in the
        // `extra_compatible_lora_families` registry (sceneworks-core `lora_family.rs`), where it
        // is now declared, and which is the same registry that lets a checkpoint DETECTED as
        // `wan-video` satisfy this declared family (it is, byte-for-byte).
        "families": ["krea-realtime"],
        "types": ["style", "motion", "character", "enhance"]
      },
      "mlx": {
        // Turnkey pre-converted MLX repo (no requiresConversion). The worker resolves env
        // override (SCENEWORKS_MLX_KREA_REALTIME_DIR) -> app-managed <data>/models/mlx/
        // krea_realtime -> this download-on-first-use snapshot, mirroring bernini/scail2.
        //
        // minMemoryGb 64 — a CLOSED DERIVATION as of sc-8446 (S13), measured on-device. It keeps the
        // same value it had as a placeholder, but for a materially different reason.
        //
        // MEASURED (Apple M5 Max, Q4 tier, 832x480 / 81 frames / 24 fps, shipped Self-Forcing
        // schedule). Cumulative MLX **active** peak by phase:
        //     through the first denoise step   9.6 GiB
        //     after the AR denoise loop       17.4 GiB
        //     after the terminal VAE decode   27.9 GiB   <- the whole-run ceiling
        // The AR plateau closes against the components: Q4 DiT ~7.8 + KV 7.14 + VAE ~0.5 = ~15.4 GiB.
        // The KV figure is measured, not modelled: 40 layers x 9360 tokens (a 6-latent-frame window at
        // 1560 tok/frame) x 40 heads x 128 dims x {k,v} x bf16 = 7.14 GiB, flat from chunk 2 on. It
        // holds ACTIVATIONS, so it does not shrink with the weight tier.
        //
        // The transient this comment used to hedge against is now quantified, and it is SMALLER than
        // feared: the VAE decode adds +10.5 GiB over the AR loop, not the ~2x assumed from the scail2
        // case. It also does not grow with clip length (33- and 81-frame clips both peak at 27.6-27.9
        // GiB), because the decode is temporally tiled.
        //
        // So what justifies 64 is the TIER LADDER, not the decode. A single per-model floor must admit
        // the heaviest INSTALLABLE tier, and bf16 swaps a 7.8 GiB DiT for a 26.6 GiB one (exact hosted
        // byte counts) => ~47 GiB active peak. 64 covers that with OS/app headroom. Per tier it would
        // be Q4 ~40 / Q8 ~48 / bf16 64 — the per-download `footprint` blocks now carry those numbers;
        // this single field cannot express them.
        //
        // ⚠️ One caveat plus sc-15325's settled decode result:
        //   * They are MLX **active**. The allocator's reclaimable buffer cache took the same run to
        //     ~90 GiB on a 128 GB host. Active is the right basis for a floor (MLX frees cache under
        //     pressure), but `get_peak_memory` must never be quoted as "the memory this uses".
        //   * sc-15325 (inference PR #292 / 51d65a1a) added spatial tiling to the corrected decode
        //     policy. The decode-phase floor fell from ~20 GiB to ~7.5 GiB (192 px spatial tile,
        //     +0.17/255), so the measured 27.90 / 34.44 / 46.71 GiB ladder is conservative rather
        //     than at risk of a raise.
        //
        // The 720p buckets add ~9.4 GiB of KV (3600 vs 1560 tokens/frame) on top of any tier — that,
        // not the weight tier, is the memory-relevant resolution jump. The UMT5 text encoder (11.4 GB
        // bf16) is NOT additive: loaded, used and freed before the DiT stages, and floored to Q8
        // (~5.7 GB) whenever the DiT tier is quantized, so it never sets the peak on q4/q8.

        "repo": "SceneWorks/krea-realtime-14b-mlx",
        // Declares Q4 as this model's default tier, matching the `default: true` q4 download.
        // ⚠️ This key is DOCUMENTATION on a video entry, not the mechanism. `mlx.quantize` is read
        // by the IMAGE lane (`resolve_quant`, image_jobs/base.rs); the video lane never consults it
        // — and deliberately so. sc-10859 made the video lane's Q4-first default an owned carve-out
        // from the app-wide Q8 default (there is no MLX video Q8 lever, so a silent Q8 only risks a
        // video-runtime OOM), and letting a manifest key flip it would quietly undo that. What
        // actually picks the tier is `krea_realtime_tier_order` (`video_jobs/krea_realtime.rs`,
        // sc-15258), which reads `advanced.mlxQuantize` through the shared `resolve_mlx_dense_quant`
        // and independently defaults to `q4`-first, then descends into the installed `<tier>/`
        // subdir. The 4 here therefore AGREES with the resolver rather than driving it; a future
        // edit to this number would NOT move the video lane. (bernini and scail2_14b declare the
        // same thing for the same reason.)
        "quantize": 4,
        "limits": {
          "samplers": ["default"],
          "schedulers": ["default"]
        }
      },
      "ui": {
        "label": "Krea Realtime 14B",
        "description": "Krea Realtime 14B: a Wan 2.1 14B backbone distilled with Self-Forcing into an autoregressive video generator. It renders frame-blocks left-to-right against a rolling causal KV cache at ~6 steps per block, so it produces a finished clip in far fewer total denoises than a whole-clip 14B diffusion. Text-to-video, image-to-video (a reference still warms the cache), and video-to-video (a source clip drives the init). macOS native MLX only; CFG-off, so there is no negative prompt or guidance control.",
        "recommendedFor": ["text_to_video", "video_to_video"],
        "durationHint": "Generates at 24fps on a 3-frame autoregressive block lattice — every duration here lands on a whole number of blocks (2s = 45 frames, 5s = 117). Keep clips at 5s or less: the shipped checkpoint has no attention sink, so longer clips drift.",
        "promptGuide": {
          "title": "Krea Realtime 14B Prompt Guide",
          "path": "/prompt-guides/krea-realtime.md",
          "sources": [
            { "label": "Krea Realtime 14B model card", "url": "https://huggingface.co/krea/krea-realtime-video" },
            { "label": "Krea Realtime technical blog post", "url": "https://www.krea.ai/blog/krea-realtime-14b" },
            { "label": "krea-ai/realtime-video inference code", "url": "https://github.com/krea-ai/realtime-video" }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    // MiniMax-H3 / Hailuo 3.0 (epic 17137, sc-17158): a flow-matching DiT that generates video AND
    // synchronized stereo audio JOINTLY — the render delivers one muxed mp4, the audio is not a
    // separate pass. Two DiT partitions ship, and they are two manifest entries (sc-17139 AC4):
    // `minimax_h3` (the `transformer` checkpoint — t2va + fl2va) and `minimax_h3_ref` (the
    // `transformer_ref` checkpoint — Ref2VA). They share one hosted repo and one shared component
    // floor; only the primary `files` predicate differs.
    //
    // ⚠️ GEOMETRY: the epic's R7 and sc-17158's own TITLE say frames are `4k + 1`. THAT IS WRONG and
    // measured false (sc-17242 spike, against the official diffusers `MiniMaxH3` on real weights;
    // correction of record on sc-17158 activity 18824 and epic activity 19108). The lattice is
    // `17n + 5`, the two are NOT nested, and the 5–15 s clamp leaves exactly FOURTEEN legal frame
    // counts, 124…345 — see `MINIMAX_H3_LEGAL_FRAME_COUNTS` in
    // crates/sceneworks-core/src/video_request.rs, which is where the app enforces it.
    //
    // ⚠️ COST: this is the most expensive video model in the catalog by a wide margin. Measured
    // 50-step projections on real weights (sc-17152, sc-17150 activity 19106):
    //     576x320  @ 124 f (5.1667 s)  →  16.26 s/step  →   813 s   (13.5 min)
    //     576x320  @ 345 f (14.375 s)  →  51.59 s/step  →  2 579 s  (43 min)
    //     1344x768 @ 124 f (5.1667 s)  → 147.87 s/step  →  7 394 s  (2.05 HOURS)
    //     1344x768 @ 345 f (14.375 s)  →  ~8.4 hours of attention alone
    // The binding constraint is a canvas x duration PRODUCT and no scalar limit can express it, so
    // `recommendedMaxDuration` stays at the hard bound and the pairing is surfaced in `ui`
    // (sc-17153 owns the fit gate that can actually reason about it).
    //
    // ⚠️ NOT SHIPPED UPSTREAM (do not imply otherwise in copy — sc-17162): `H3-Context-IR` (hosted
    // prompt refinement, so prompt adherence differs from the Hailuo API), `H3-Regenerate-2K` (so
    // "up to 2K" is NOT reachable from these weights). Dense attention remains the default and
    // the basis of the cost table above; optional native Veda uses its managed predictor.
    //
    // ⚠️ NO IMAGE LANE. `min_duration = 5.0` is hardcoded upstream, so `T = 1` does not render at
    // all. The epic's image-lane premise ("T=1 collapses ~31k tokens to ~1k") has no basis;
    // sc-17240 / sc-17241 / sc-18666 are in Backlog and must not be revived from this entry.
    {
      "id": "minimax_h3",
      "name": "MiniMax-H3",
      "family": "minimax-h3",
      "type": "video",
      "adapter": "minimax_h3",
      // t2va (text → audio+video) and fl2va (first and/or last frame conditioning, 0/1/2 images).
      // Ref2VA lives on `minimax_h3_ref`, which is the only partition whose checkpoint conditions
      // on multi-modal references. Reachability (VIDEO_UI_MODES eligibility, the
      // `validate_video_job` allow-list, the worker's routing) is sc-17159 — declaration is NOT
      // reachability (GH #2074), and this entry deliberately claims only what the checkpoint does.
      "capabilities": ["text_to_video", "image_to_video", "first_last_frame"],
      // sc-17242 spike, measured against the official diffusers reference: the pipeline's ENTIRE
      // input surface is 19 params — `prompt`, `image`, `last_image`, `references`, `num_frames`,
      // `height`, `width`, `num_inference_steps`, `generator`, `latents`, `audio_latents`,
      // `output_type`, `attention_kwargs` and 6 intermediates. There is **no `guidance_scale` and no
      // `negative_prompt`**, so both axes are declared false and Video Studio hides the controls
      // rather than offering knobs the worker would discard. ABSENT would mean TRUE here (the video
      // block's polarity is the opposite of `audio`'s), which is why silence is not an option.
      "video": {
        "supportsSchedulerShift": true, "supportsGuidance": false, "supportsNegativePrompt": false },
      // sc-17227 — LICENCE ACKNOWLEDGMENT WITHOUT A CREDENTIAL. `gated` is deliberately ABSENT:
      // `MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3` is a PUBLIC repo, so there is no Hugging Face token to add and no
      // "Request access" page to visit. Declaring `gated: true` here to get the acknowledgment
      // checkbox would demand a credential that does not exist. `requiresLicenseAcknowledgment` is
      // the decoupled half: it raises the in-app acknowledgment gate (sc-7872) and blocks the
      // download until the user accepts, with no credential UI.
      //
      // WHY IT IS REQUIRED AT ALL. The MiniMax H3 Community License is non-transferable (§II) and
      // defines a "Licensee" as whoever uses the Works (§I.9), so the user is a Licensee in their
      // own right — SceneWorks' authorization does not stand in for theirs. §V.2 obliges us, before
      // providing access, to notify each user that the §V / Exhibit A restrictions apply. This is
      // that notification; `licenseNotice` is its text. It does NOT resolve the §V.4 territorial
      // question — an acknowledgment cannot make an Excluded-Territory use authorized — which stays
      // Michael's ship/no-ship call on sc-17227. Restored (sc-17227 review MINOR 5): deleting it
      // left this file contradicting item 2 of docs/minimax-h3-use-restriction-safeguards.md, which
      // still says exactly this and is owned by sc-19541.
      "requiresLicenseAcknowledgment": true,
      "licenseUrl": "https://huggingface.co/MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3",
      "licenseNotice": "MiniMax-H3's weights, and the video and audio they produce, are governed by the MiniMax H3 Community License Agreement — not by SceneWorks' own AGPL-3.0 licence. That agreement grants a NON-TRANSFERABLE licence (§II) and defines a \"Licensee\" as whoever uses the model (§I.9), so it binds you directly and SceneWorks' authorization from MiniMax does not stand in for yours. Four of its terms decide whether you may use this model at all. (1) APPLICABLE TERRITORY (§I.3, §I.5, §V.4): the agreement's default Applicable Territory is worldwide excluding the European Union, the United Kingdom, the Republic of Korea, and the United States of America. Separately, MiniMax has confirmed in writing that it authorizes SceneWorks to use MiniMax H3 and MiniMax H3 Works, conditioned on SceneWorks' continued compliance with the commitments in its request; which provision of the agreement that confirmation is given under is not stated here. Questions about your own use go to MiniMax at api@minimax.io (§IV.1). (2) ACCEPTABLE USE POLICY (§V.1 and Exhibit A): twenty prohibited uses, of which item 12 requires you to clearly and prominently disclose that content is machine-generated whenever you publish it to a public environment — SceneWorks does not write that disclosure into your files, so making it is yours to do. (3) ADDITIONAL COMMERCIAL TERMS (§IV.1): separate prior written authorization from MiniMax is required if your commercial products and services generate more than 20 million US dollars in yearly revenue. (4) NO IMPROVING OTHER AI MODELS (§V.3): you may not use the Works or any of their Outputs or results to improve any other artificial intelligence model, other than MiniMax H3 or its Model Derivatives. SceneWorks ships a LoRA trainer, dataset captioning and a training studio, so this is the restriction the product's own features are most likely to reach — training a different model on MiniMax-H3 renders is what §V.3 forbids. Read the full text under About → Licenses before downloading.",
      "downloads": [
        // sc-25773 — optional learned attention predictor; dense H3 stays usable without it.
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "Veda-Sparse/Minimax-H3-T2VA-Veda-8NFE-600Step-Preview",
