        // the default canvas is already two hours. Recommending a shorter scalar would falsely
        // imply long clips are infeasible everywhere (sc-17152).
        "recommendedMaxDuration": 14.375,
        // 🔴 THE UNIT IS MODEL EVALUATIONS (NFE), and it is the same unit everywhere: `defaults.steps`
        // above, `advanced.steps`, this floor, and the `sampling.steps` each turbo adapter declares
        // in builtin.loras.jsonc. The engine builds the sigma grid as `linspace(1, 0, steps + 1)` —
        // it appends the terminal 0 ITSELF (`JointSchedule::with_shifts(evaluations + 1, ..)`) — so
        // `defaults.steps: 50` really is 50 evaluations and nothing in SceneWorks adds or subtracts
        // one. sc-18726 asked for that call to be made once and tested; it is pinned by
        // `minimax_h3_turbo_steps_are_model_evaluations_not_grid_points` (sceneworks-core) and by
        // the arm-level `a_selected_turbo_variant_changes_the_job_that_reaches_the_engine`.
        //
        // ⚠️ This comment previously read "N steps drive N-1 model evaluations" and concluded that
        // `steps: 1` is unrenderable. That describes diffusers' `num_inference_steps`, which is NOT
        // what this key carries: at 1 NFE the engine builds a legal 2-point grid and renders. So the
        // floor is a PRODUCT judgement, not an engine limit — a 1-evaluation schedule is a single
        // Euler jump from pure noise, which is not a fast draft, it is noise. 2 is the smallest
        // schedule with an intermediate sigma. REFUSED rather than quietly raised, the same
        // reject-never-clamp posture the duration bounds take (sc-19426, corrected sc-18726).
        "hardMinSteps": 2,
        // One cadence. The lattice, the duration window and every measured render are 24 fps.
        "fps": [24],
        // `CANVAS_MAX_PIXELS` = 1,032,192 = 768 x 1344, and it is ENFORCED as an AREA budget. It had
        // been declared and unused, and `Capabilities::max_size` bounds each edge INDEPENDENTLY, so
        // 1344x1344 previously passed every check at 1.75x the checkpoint's budget (sc-17152). Every
        // advertised bucket below satisfies the AREA, not merely the per-edge cap.
        "maxPixels": 1032192,
        // NO `requiresDimensionsMultipleOf`. `SIZE_MULTIPLE` is 32 (the VAE's 16x spatial stride x
        // the DiT's width patch 2), which is already core's default floor — the same reason
        // wan_2_2 and scail2_14b declare nothing. Declaring 32 would be a no-op that reads as a
        // per-model choice; `dimension_multiple_of` is pinned at 32 for this id by
        // `shipped_manifest_matches_each_engines_real_geometry`.
        //
        // There is NO `aspect_ratio` parameter upstream — the canvas derives from the first
        // keyframe's ratio, or defaults to 16:9 with no keyframe, and bounds are 1:4 to 4:1. These
        // buckets are the MEASURED `resolve_canvas_size` outputs (21:9 → 1536x672, 16:9 → 1344x768,
        // 1:1 → 768x768, 9:16 → 768x1344, 4:3 → 1024x768) plus their transposes, and the 576x320
        // pair sc-17152 rendered on. The 4:1 edge case resolves to 2016x512, which is past the app's
        // own 1920 px per-side clamp, so it is deliberately NOT advertised — an advertised bucket
        // must be a fixed point. A keyframe is STRETCHED onto the canvas, not letterboxed, and the
        // resolved ratio drifts slightly (a 4:1 request comes back 3.938:1).
        "resolutions": ["1536x672", "672x1536", "1344x768", "768x1344", "1024x768", "768x1024", "768x768", "576x320", "320x576"],
        // t2va and fl2va condition on `sourceAssetId` / `lastFrameAssetId`, never on the reference
        // LISTS — this partition's checkpoint has no reference-conditioning path at all, so all
        // three are 0 and an engine can never be handed conditioning it would silently ignore
        // (which is invisible in the output). `minimax_h3_ref` is where 9 / 3 / 3 / 12 lives.
        "maxReferenceAssets": 0,
        "maxSourceClipAssets": 0,
        "maxReferenceAudioAssets": 0,
        // Default-only. The permanent inference pin (`28f0563b`) makes the descriptor reachable, so
        // the engines.rs drift guard can compare a curated menu against MiniMax-H3's real
        // capabilities — but the provider advertises `samplers: ["default"]` and no scheduler menu
        // of its own, so there is nothing to curate INTO. This stays `["default"]` because that is
        // what the engine offers, not because the descriptor is invisible; a curated menu is
        // additive if the engine ever grows one.
        "samplers": ["default"],
        "schedulers": ["default"]
      },
      "mlx": {
        // 64 — the DERIVED floor for the DEFAULT (q4) tier (sc-17153), replacing a 96 that went
        // stale the day sc-19120 merged. NOT a measured end-to-end ceiling: no per-tier
        // calibration receipt exists for this family yet (`mlx.calibrations` is absent —
        // deliberately, because inventing one here would read as evidence), and producing those
        // receipts, each tier in its own process, belongs to epic 17137's single terminal
        // measurement campaign. Until that lands, this scalar is the honest derivation OF today's
        // measurements, not a measurement itself.
        //
        // ⚠️ WHY 96 WAS WRONG. It priced the DENSE Qwen3-VL-32B text encoder's 53.07 GB isolation
        // peak (sc-17150 activity 19121) — a stage the DEFAULT tier no longer runs. sc-19120's
        // packed q4 conditioning stage measures 14.68 GB end to end (inference
        // `mlx-gen-minimax-h3/src/memory_strategy.rs` + `tests/te_tier_generate_stages.rs`, read
        // at permanent inference pin `28f0563b`), so 96 over-gated the default tier by ~38 GB at the derivation
        // geometry and locked the family off every sub-96 GB Mac for no measured reason.
        //
        // THE DERIVATION — max over the shipped q4 pipeline's stages at the LARGEST geometry this
        // entry admits (the 1,032,192-px canvas at 14.375 s / 345 frames ≈ 104k packed rows;
        // the writable-elements crossing near 260 frames is a memory cost, not a gate — inference
        // `cost.rs` module docs):
        //   * conditioning (packed q4 TE): 14.68 GB measured, geometry-invariant on the video axes;
        //   * denoise: 11.63 GB q4 DiT resident (post AdaLN-evict, measured) + a compute-dtype
        //     (bf16, tier-independent) transient that is LINEAR in packed rows — every tensor the
        //     forward MATERIALIZES is linear in S (the SwiGLU projection is the widest; fused SDPA
        //     streams the S² scores, `cost.rs`) — pinned by two points: ~1.1 GB at ~3.3k rows
        //     (12.75 GB stage total, te_tier_generate_stages) and ≤12.6 GB at 37.8k rows (the bf16
        //     sweep's 52.81 GB peak at 1344x768/124f minus the 40.43 GB bf16 resident; the stage
        //     never broke the dense-TE mask, sc-17152 / activity 18976). Extrapolated to the
        //     ~104k-row envelope top: ~35 GB transient ⇒ ~46.5 GB stage peak. THIS STAGE BINDS.
        //   * decode: 5.77 GB measured at the small anchor; spatially tiled (rung 2, frozen
        //     256/64 px pair), so growth is latent/output buffers (~4 GB at the top), far under
        //     the denoise stage. The VAEs are untiered, so this is tier-independent.
        // Wired ceiling is `recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize` (~75 % of unified memory): 46.5 / 0.75 ≈
        // 62 ⇒ a 64 GB Mac (~48 GB ceiling) holds the whole admitted surface; a 48 GB Mac
        // (~36 GB) does not at the envelope top. A blanket scalar cannot say "48 GB runs the
        // smaller geometries" — that is the REQUEST-scoped fit gate's job, not this floor's.
        //
        // NOT priced in, on purpose: sc-18662's streamed rung-4 request (requestPeak 5.80 GB q4 at
        // 124f/384x224, decode-bound) is measured at an inference revision this app does not pin —
        // the shipped pipeline holds the whole q4 DiT resident through the denoise. When the pin
        // moves and the terminal campaign measures per-tier receipts, this scalar and the
        // rung4-applicability-survey's outOfMatrixFamilies["17137"] characterization get real
        // numbers together.

        "repo": "SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx",
        "limits": {
          "samplers": ["default"],
          "schedulers": ["default"]
        }
      },
      // sc-20754 — measured on the Windows CUDA real-weights host, one tier per fresh process on an
      // idle 96 GB RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell. Every probe used the shipped 1344x768 / 124-frame / 50-NFE
      // workload, `CANDLE_GEN_OFFLOAD=sequential`, and a 0.00 GB idle baseline. `vramGbByTier` is the
      // base-10 nvidia-smi POOL high-water, not a weights-floor estimate: q4 40.068 GB, q8 74.696 GB,
      // bf16 67.012 GB. Decode owned the pool peak in all three runs; the concurrent-live driver
      // high-waters were 36.69 / 65.80 / 62.66 GiB respectively. The non-monotonic q8/bf16 ordering
      // is measured, not normalized away: the packed q8 route retained a larger allocator pool.
      //
      // `minMemoryGb` is the DEFAULT q4 measured peak plus the gate's 2 GB dedicated-VRAM reserve,
      // rounded up to the integer schema: ceil(40.068 + 2) = 43. Per-tier admission still reads each
      // exact row and adds the same reserve, so q8 and bf16 are never sized against this q4 floor.
      // Routing remains a separate catalog decision (GH #2074); this block records backend memory
      // evidence and does not itself make a Candle route reachable.
      "candle": {

      },
      "ui": {
        "label": "MiniMax-H3",
        // sc-17227 — §IV.2 of the MiniMax H3 Community License: "You shall prominently display
        // 'MiniMax H3' on the user interface of commercial product or service that uses MiniMax H3
        // or MiniMax H3 Works." The exact string with a SPACE, not the hyphenated repo name, and
        // rendered as its own line above the description on the model card rather than folded into
        // prose. §III.3.a separately encourages the "Powered by MiniMax H3" wording, so one line
        // discharges both.
        "attribution": "Powered by MiniMax H3",
        // sc-17162 — THE HONESTY PASS OVER THE WITHHELD UPSTREAM COMPONENTS, in the one place a
        // user reads before committing to a ~30 GB install (`ModelManagerScreen.jsx` renders this
        // as the model card body; it is `ui.description`'s ONLY reader). Four claims here are
        // load-bearing rather than descriptive, and each is checkable:
        //   1. NOT the hosted product — `H3-Context-IR` and `H3-Regenerate-2K` are unreleased, so
        //      prompt adherence differs from the Hailuo API and 2K is unreachable. The load-bearing
        //      enforcement for THAT claim is `CANVAS_MAX_PIXELS` (1,032,192), the AREA budget: a 2K
        //      frame is >2x it on area alone, so no per-edge value can make 2K reachable and there
        //      is no 2K path to be coy about. See inference
        //      `docs/reference/minimax-h3-withheld-upstream-components.md`.
        //
        //      ⚠️ DO NOT cite `max_size: 1344` as the 2K argument, which this comment previously
        //      did. It read as a contradiction against this entry's own `limits.resolutions`, which
        //      advertises `1536x672` / `672x1536` — 1536 > 1344 on the long edge — so the same PR
        //      appeared to assert a 1344 per-edge ceiling and a 1536-wide bucket at once. The area
        //      budget is the argument that actually carries the 2K claim and is not in tension with
        //      anything, so it is the one stated above.
        //
        //      The per-edge cap is a SEPARATE, independently-applied bound, and it currently sits
        //      BELOW this entry's advertised 21:9 pair. So `1536x672` / `672x1536` are ADVERTISED
        //      BUT NOT YET RENDERABLE: the area check passes and the edge check refuses.
        //
        //      ✅ DISCHARGED BY THE PERMANENT INFERENCE PIN. `Cargo.toml` pins
        //      `28f0563baa03640ade1635356d2d54fe8a477f1a`; the pinned provider's per-edge ceiling
        //      is `MAX_CANVAS_EDGE = 2016` on both lanes, comfortably above 1536, so the engine
        //      accepts the pair and the paragraph above is HISTORY, kept only because it explains
        //      why the area budget alone never settled the question.
        //
        //      The tie is enforced, not asserted in prose: `pinned_engine_geometry.rs` reads the
        //      PINNED provider's own `capabilities.max_size` and refuses any advertised resolution
        //      whose long edge exceeds it, so a pin that moved backwards would go red in Rust
        //      instead of silently re-advertising a canvas the engine refuses. The prompt guide's
        //      *Sizes* section carries the pair with no caveat, pinned by
        //      `minimaxH3CatalogCopy.test.jsx`.
        //
        //      Do NOT write the new ceiling's VALUE here or in the copy. It is an engine internal
        //      that dates this comment the moment the resolver moves, and 1536 — the intuitive
        //      guess, being this pair's own long edge — is WRONG: the resolver emits wider still at
        //      the 4:1 aspect ceiling, so a 1536 cap would keep refusing a canvas the engine
        //      produces on its own. #640 carries a guard that reds specifically on that value.
        //
        //      This repo now CHECKS all of it. `PinnedAreaCap::NotInThePinnedBundle` is gone with
        //      sc-19721's pin bump: `pinned_engine_geometry.rs` imports
        //      `providers::minimax_h3::pipeline` and ties `maxPixels` to the engine's own
        //      `CANVAS_MAX_PIXELS` and every advertised resolution's long edge to the registered
        //      descriptor's `max_size`. A manifest that got ahead of the engine, or a pin that
        //      moved behind the manifest, is red there rather than a job-time reject.
        //   2. Dense attention by default; optional Veda stages the managed learned predictor.
        //      The dense cost table does not set the 14.38 s
        //      ceiling: that comes from the checkpoint's own 5–15 s clamp meeting the `17n + 5`
        //      lattice, which sparse attention does not touch. Do not conflate the two in copy.
        //   3. 15 s is REFUSED, not shortened. `limits.hardMaxDuration` is 14.375 and the next
        //      lattice rung is 15.083 s, so a 15 s request errors — a user who reads "5–15 s" on
        //      MiniMax's own page and plans around it needs to learn that HERE, not from a failed
        //      submit two hours into a queue.
        //   4. No still-image mode — `min_duration = 5.0` is hardcoded upstream, so `T = 1` does
        //      not render at all.
        // The `<d>` dialogue markers (the fourth withheld surface) are a PROMPTING detail and live
        // in the prompt guide, which is the only place a user would copy that syntax from.
        "description": "MiniMax-H3 (Hailuo 3.0) generates video and synchronized stereo audio together in one pass — the clip arrives with its soundtrack already muxed, not dubbed on afterwards. Text-to-video plus first and/or last frame conditioning; there is no still-image mode, because the checkpoint refuses anything shorter than about five seconds. Clips come in fourteen fixed lengths between 5.17s and 14.38s at 24fps and nothing in between renders — including the 15s MiniMax's own documentation advertises, which is refused outright rather than quietly shortened to 14.38s. This is the open-weights model, not the hosted Hailuo product: the prompt-understanding front end (H3-Context-IR) and the 2K in-context upscaler (H3-Regenerate-2K) are not released, so prompt adherence differs from the API and 2K is not reachable from these weights. Attention runs dense by default. Optional Veda sparse attention uses the managed predictor and falls back to dense when it cannot run; the shortest dense clip at the default 1344x768 canvas is about a two-hour render, while the whole length range stays comfortable at 576x320. There is no guidance scale and no negative prompt — the model has neither axis, so those controls are hidden rather than missing. It renders through native MLX on Apple Silicon and Candle/CUDA on Windows and Linux; the measured q4, q8, and bf16 tiers require roughly 42 GB, 77 GB, and 69 GB of free VRAM respectively after the safety reserve.",
        "recommendedFor": ["text_to_video", "image_to_video", "first_last_frame"],
        "durationHint": "Fourteen clip lengths only, 5.17s to 14.38s at 24fps — the frame lattice is 17n+5, so nothing in between renders, and 15s is refused rather than shortened to 14.38s. Cost is driven by CANVAS far more than by length: 5.17s at 576x320 is ~14 minutes and the whole 14.38s range is comfortable there, while 5.17s at the default 1344x768 is already ~2 hours. Pick the small canvas first, then the length.",
        "promptGuide": {
          "title": "MiniMax-H3 Prompt Guide",
          "path": "/prompt-guides/minimax-h3.md",
          "sources": [
            { "label": "MiniMax-H3 model card", "url": "https://huggingface.co/MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3" },
            { "label": "Hailuo 3.0 prompting guide", "url": "https://hailuoai.video/" },
            { "label": "MiniMax platform video generation docs", "url": "https://platform.minimax.io/docs/api-reference/video-generation" }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    // The Ref2VA partition. Same architecture, same geometry, same shared floor — a DIFFERENT
    // checkpoint (`transformer_ref`), which is the only one that conditions on multi-modal
    // references. A second ENTRY rather than a co-requisite of the first (sc-17139 AC4), because
    // `variant` is a closed quant enum with no "which checkpoint" axis and the two are separately
    // pickable in the Studios. Same shape as the wan_2_2_t2v_14b / wan_2_2_i2v_14b pair.
    //
    // ⚠️ AMENDED BY sc-19573. sc-17139 AC4 additionally argued that a hard co-requisite "would force
    // +18.78 GB on every q4 text-to-video user". That premise was wrong: `mlx-gen-minimax-h3::load`
    // opens BOTH partitions on every load, so those bytes were always part of the minimum loadable
    // set and withholding them produced an install that rendered nothing. Each entry now declares
    // the OTHER partition as a per-tier co-requisite (see the rows in both `downloads` arrays); the
    // entries stay separate, the bytes stop being optional. `required: "soft"` was the other option
    // and is wrong here for the reason sc-17139 gave — a soft co-requisite keeps the primary usable,
    // which is precisely the state that does not exist for this pair.
    //
    // The two entries share the primary repo `SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx` and differ only in the
    // per-tier `files` predicate, so a per-tier delete on one reclaims exactly its own subtree.
    // (Whole-model delete resolves the repo cache path, so removing either entry can strand the
    // other's bytes — a pre-existing, repo-wide characteristic of the twelve shared-repo groups
    // already in this catalog, e.g. bernini / bernini_image, not something this pair introduces.)
    {
      "id": "minimax_h3_ref",
      "name": "MiniMax-H3 References",
      "family": "minimax-h3",
      "type": "video",
      "adapter": "minimax_h3",
      // Ref2VA only: up to 9 images, 3 video clips and 3 audio clips, 12 files in total. It is not
      // a text-to-video or first/last-frame checkpoint — those are `minimax_h3`.
      "capabilities": ["reference_to_video"],
      // sc-17242 spike, measured against the official diffusers reference: the pipeline's ENTIRE
      // input surface is 19 params — `prompt`, `image`, `last_image`, `references`, `num_frames`,
      // `height`, `width`, `num_inference_steps`, `generator`, `latents`, `audio_latents`,
      // `output_type`, `attention_kwargs` and 6 intermediates. There is **no `guidance_scale` and no
      // `negative_prompt`**, so both axes are declared false and Video Studio hides the controls
      // rather than offering knobs the worker would discard. ABSENT would mean TRUE here (the video
      // block's polarity is the opposite of `audio`'s), which is why silence is not an option.
      "video": {
        "supportsSchedulerShift": true, "supportsGuidance": false, "supportsNegativePrompt": false },
      // sc-17227 — same licence gate as `minimax_h3`, and it must be declared HERE too: the
      // acknowledgment is keyed per model id, and this is a separate entry the user can install
      // on its own. See the sibling entry for why `gated` is absent (the HF repo is public, so
      // there is no credential to demand) and why §V.2 requires the notification.
      "requiresLicenseAcknowledgment": true,
      "licenseUrl": "https://huggingface.co/MiniMaxAI/MiniMax-H3",
      "licenseNotice": "MiniMax-H3's weights, and the video and audio they produce, are governed by the MiniMax H3 Community License Agreement — not by SceneWorks' own AGPL-3.0 licence. That agreement grants a NON-TRANSFERABLE licence (§II) and defines a \"Licensee\" as whoever uses the model (§I.9), so it binds you directly and SceneWorks' authorization from MiniMax does not stand in for yours. Four of its terms decide whether you may use this model at all. (1) APPLICABLE TERRITORY (§I.3, §I.5, §V.4): the agreement's default Applicable Territory is worldwide excluding the European Union, the United Kingdom, the Republic of Korea, and the United States of America. Separately, MiniMax has confirmed in writing that it authorizes SceneWorks to use MiniMax H3 and MiniMax H3 Works, conditioned on SceneWorks' continued compliance with the commitments in its request; which provision of the agreement that confirmation is given under is not stated here. Questions about your own use go to MiniMax at api@minimax.io (§IV.1). (2) ACCEPTABLE USE POLICY (§V.1 and Exhibit A): twenty prohibited uses, of which item 12 requires you to clearly and prominently disclose that content is machine-generated whenever you publish it to a public environment — SceneWorks does not write that disclosure into your files, so making it is yours to do. (3) ADDITIONAL COMMERCIAL TERMS (§IV.1): separate prior written authorization from MiniMax is required if your commercial products and services generate more than 20 million US dollars in yearly revenue. (4) NO IMPROVING OTHER AI MODELS (§V.3): you may not use the Works or any of their Outputs or results to improve any other artificial intelligence model, other than MiniMax H3 or its Model Derivatives. SceneWorks ships a LoRA trainer, dataset captioning and a training studio, so this is the restriction the product's own features are most likely to reach — training a different model on MiniMax-H3 renders is what §V.3 forbids. Read the full text under About → Licenses before downloading.",
      "downloads": [
        // sc-25773 — optional learned attention predictor; dense H3 stays usable without it.
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "Veda-Sparse/Minimax-H3-T2VA-Veda-8NFE-600Step-Preview",
          "revision": "9a1fd3a41b4a754a7886e64e82edbddf599fd1bd",
          "files": ["minimax_h3_t2va_veda_8nfe_600step_preview_fp8.safetensors"],
          "expectedSizeBytes": 275415648,
          "expectedSha256": "2a8d8845c5342756a2781e8e69563940e4bb573c9a40ebb534915ff8fd76573a",
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 275415648,
          "coRequisite": true,
          "required": "soft",
          "componentId": "veda_predictor",
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"]
        },

        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx",
          "revision": "137ce668c55a20bc0935fd1cf2a3de8448abb7f4",
          "variant": "q4",
          "default": true,
          "files": ["q4/transformer_ref/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          // `transformer_ref` is byte-for-byte the same SIZE as `transformer` at every tier — the
          // two carry identical `config.json` and the same 638 tensor names, so a name-comparison
          // check cannot tell them apart; they are different WEIGHTS.
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 18780109783,
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 18780109783 }
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx",
          "revision": "137ce668c55a20bc0935fd1cf2a3de8448abb7f4",
          "variant": "q8",
          "files": ["q8/transformer_ref/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 35302064357,
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 35302064357 }
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx",
          "revision": "137ce668c55a20bc0935fd1cf2a3de8448abb7f4",
          "variant": "bf16",
          "files": ["bf16/transformer_ref/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 66280569250,
          "footprint": { "diskSizeBytes": 66280569250 }
        },
        // BYTE-IDENTICAL to `minimax_h3`'s co-requisite rows — same repos, same pinned revisions,
        // same component ids, same file predicates, same `variant` scoping. That identity is what
        // makes the shared floor download ONCE for both entries instead of twice; a co-requisite is
        // never removed when one of the models that references it is deleted. The floor is now
        // TIER-SCOPED rather than a single number: 72.9 GB at bf16, but 29.7 GB at q4 and 44.7 GB at
        // q8, because sc-19120 made the text encoder a per-tier component (see `minimax_h3`).
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx",
          "revision": "137ce668c55a20bc0935fd1cf2a3de8448abb7f4",
          "variant": "q4",
          "coRequisite": true,
          "componentId": "text_encoder",
          "subdir": "q4/text_encoder",
          "files": ["q4/text_encoder/*"],
          "platforms": ["macos", "windows", "linux"],
          "estimatedSizeBytes": 18722713964
        },
        {
          "provider": "huggingface",
          "repo": "SceneWorks/minimax-h3-mlx",
          "revision": "137ce668c55a20bc0935fd1cf2a3de8448abb7f4",
          "variant": "q8",
          "coRequisite": true,
